when you think of summer grilling you  usually think of savory foods  but you can also make really great  desserts right on the grill we're gonna  focus on two of the most popular grilled  desserts a cobbler and a crumble  sure you can make these in the oven but  let's talk about why you should be  grilling your desserts  listen it's summertime it's hot you  don't want to make your kitchen even  hotter by turning that oven on if you're  having a summer barbecue you don't want  to be going back and forth inside  outside get outside hang out with your  friends hang out with your family get  grilling  first up we're going to show you how to  make a versatile fruity filling that you  can use with a bunch of desserts the  great thing about these recipes is that  the filling's the same  your main components are going to be  sugar a binder  salt vanilla acid and fruit first  combine sugar  next up for our binder we tested flour  cornstarch and tapioca  turns out they all had really similar  results the recipe already calls for  flour it works just as well next add a  bit of salt it helps enhance the flavor  of all the other ingredients in baked  goods  then you're ready to add your acid in  our case we're using lemon juice and  zest next add your vanilla place your  vanilla bean on a cutting board you're  going to use a parry knife to slowly  gently split it in half  and then take one half of the pod  and scrape all the seeds that's the  stuff you're gonna save  you could use an extract but if you  spring for the real deal you'll get an  extra punch of flavor then you're ready  to add your fruit for this filling  you'll need 8 cups of any fruit you  could do a classic strawberry rhubarb  mixed berries or really anything that's  in season we're going with blueberries  and peaches you can also use frozen  fruit you'll get a pretty similar result  but why not take advantage of summer  produce if you want to take it up a  notch you can also grill your fruit  it does take some extra time so you can  totally skip this step but it will add  some extra flavor  now it's really simple you're just going  to give it a quick stir you want to make  sure the fruit is fully covered with the  sugar mixture now that that's ready  transfer your filling into your dish or  cookware for your cookware you could use  stainless steel metal or cast iron you  just want to make sure that whatever you  use it's grill friendly for this recipe  we're using a standard 10 inch cast iron  skillet but you could adapt this for  whatever cookware you have at home add  your filling to the cookware you want to  make sure there's a little extra room on  top so that the fruit doesn't bubble  over and overflow  now we're going to set that aside while  we prep our topping it's totally fine to  sit in the fridge for a few hours or up  to overnight the easiest topping to  round off this dessert is a crumble  for the crumble you're going to use  flour  sugar oats butter  salt and spices  you could also make a crisp by just  taking out the oats they're both pretty  similar so it's just up to you with  which one you prefer this one's super  foolproof first you mix your dry  ingredients until fully incorporated  then using your hands break the butter  up into pea sized pieces they can be a  little uneven don't worry about it you  could use a pastry cutter to cut up the  butter if you had one but we like the  rustic look you get from breaking it up  with your hands now that you have your  topping just spread it in an even layer  all over the fruit  it kind of looks like a lot but remember  there's eight cups of fruit in there you  want to make sure you have enough  topping to balance all that fruit to  cook or crumble you want to preheat the  grill to 350. you're going to cook it  over indirect heat with the lid closed  for about an hour and 30 minutes we'd  suggest checking your crumble after  about an hour  grilling isn't an exact science and  grills can be really finicky you don't  want this to burn  some people might think it's done at  this point but if you see any underbaked  flour or these really light golden parts  that means it's not ready  when it's finished it should look like  this you're looking for a golden brown  gorgeous topping and the fruity filling  to be bubbling and oozing through and  that's your crumble  for something a little more involved  kick it up a notch and try a cobbler a  lot of people top their cobbler with pie  batter for something that's a total  showstopper we're going to show you how  to make a biscuit topping for the  biscuits you'll need flour milk or cream  butter salt sugar baking powder and a  little extra sugar to sprinkle on top  first add all your dry ingredients and  give that a good whisk until they're  well combined  now we're gonna add our butter for your  butter you can totally cube it but pro  tip try grating it it's a little extra  effort but it makes it easier to combine  the butter evenly once that's set add  your milk or cream  for this recipe we tested milk heavy  cream and buttermilk they all work great  but we're going with buttermilk because  we love the tanginess it added you'll  know your dough is ready when it's got  this shaggy texture  now turn your dough out onto a floured  surface and flatten it into a rectangle  then start rolling it out for this  you're going to want to do the book end  trick take your rolling pin and roll  your rectangle out until it's a little  bigger  then  gently fold it in half roll it out again  you're gonna do this four or five times  you don't need to do more than that this  process builds the layers of butter  resulting in a super flaky tender  biscuit  now that your dough's all rolled out  it's time to cut out your biscuits you  want to take your pastry or cookie  cutter and dip that right in a bowl of  flour that'll keep it from sticking to  the dough when you're cutting out your  biscuits make sure you press straight  down without twisting your cutter back  and forth that way you'll get a clean  cut twisting it back and forth is going  to crimp the edges and your biscuits  won't be as tall and fluffy place the  biscuits on top of your fruit filling  making sure that they're all touching  lastly brush the biscuits with a little  more buttermilk milk or egg wash to give  them a nice sheen  then top them with a little bit of sugar  we're going to use raw sugar for some  extra texture but you can totally use  white sugar too it's important to keep  this in the fridge until you're ready to  grill so that the butter in your  biscuits doesn't melt resulting in a  soggy mess same as the crumble preheat  your grill to 350 degrees again you're  going to do this over indirect heat  we're going to grill this for an hour  and 15 minutes with the lid closed you  want to start checking your cobbler  early if it's starting to get too brown  just tent it with tin foil for the  remaining time when your cobbler is done  the biscuit should be golden brown on  top and the filling should be bubbly and  oozing  and that's it i know you want to eat  this right away but you really want to  let it rest for at least 30 minutes so  that the filling can set  this is a great base recipe to  experiment with and they're really  delicious i think that's the thing like  these are so good whether it's a crumble  or a cobbler feel free to top it off  with some fan favorites like ice cream  or whipped cream we love them both you  can't go wrong with a crumble or cobbler  you really can't happy grilling  vanilla extract vanilla vanilla  am i saying all right vanilla  vanilla  vanilla do ice  vanilla  vanilla  vanilla  vanilla okay it doesn't matter it's fine
 

